# Swift Development Rules

## Architecture
- MVVM with @Observable macro (macOS 15+)
- Directory structure: Engine/, Models/, Views/, Extensions/
- One file per type. File name matches type name.
- SwiftData @Model for persistence. @Query in views.

## Swift Style
- Swift 6 strict concurrency: @MainActor on view models, Sendable conformance
- Prefer structs over classes unless reference semantics needed
- camelCase for vars/funcs, PascalCase for types
- Explicit return types on public functions
- Use `import Foundation` not `import UIKit` for non-UI code

## SwiftUI
- NavigationStack, not NavigationView
- SF Symbols for all icons (SF Pro for text, SF Mono for data)
- .task {} for async work, not .onAppear with Task {}
- @Environment for dependency injection
- Native macOS controls — no custom chrome
- @Query for SwiftData fetching, never manual fetch in views

## Testing
- Swift Testing framework: @Test, @Suite, #expect
- In-memory ModelContainer for data tests
- Test behavior through public interfaces, not implementation
- One logical assertion per test
- Use UUID .id comparison, never === for SwiftData models

## Patterns to Avoid
- No === for SwiftData model comparison (use .id)
- No Timer.publish on struct views (use .task + async loop)
- No try? that silently swallows errors (use do/catch, surface via lastError)
- No @StateObject (use @State with @Observable)
- No SELECT * equivalent — fetch only what's needed

## Build Commands
- `cd Tiempo && swift build` — compile
- `cd Tiempo && swift test` — run all tests
- `cd Tiempo && swift build -c release` — release build
- `open Tiempo/.build/release/Tiempo` — launch the app
